86184282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050945 #edb/ref 1031 "Deterministic Finite Automata" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050945 #edb/ref 1033 3797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050945 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "740ffc28-6948-5a31-a34b-ff97e84388d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1031 "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1033 3001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051001 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186051501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186051502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186051503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186051504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186051505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186051502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051500 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5dd58087-ae96-5ce0-a2c8-bc32e79b782d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051501 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051501 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051501 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae566481-20f8-5e52-99d8-23a6c82691b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051502 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051502 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cdadc5ef-7415-5dcf-b7cf-505d2e49c925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051503 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051503 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051503 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d138c6e0-6807-55b7-ab39-2b7416f93002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051504 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051504 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051504 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4129157c-b81d-59dd-8ec5-f67444db60ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051505 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051505 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051505 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "574a525d-c730-52ff-a8ed-06f70442fbc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054435 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054435 #edb/ref 1071 "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\},$ $B = \\{f,g\\},$ and $C = A \\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nI. $(f,4) \\in C$\n\nII. $(7,g) \\in C$\n\nIII. $(3,f) \\in C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f2ec8a1-0ab4-55ea-9472-2dd7b9fa5f8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054435 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/68/683bad9c822cea7218b2fd8a252e46215103c95b3ee9c42e41e7141ed8a40330.png)\n\nHence, the set $C = A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{ \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054436 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054436 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A = \\{ p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4,9 \\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B?$\n\nI. $((p,4),9)$\n\nII. $((q,9),4)$\n\nIII. $(p,4,9)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a101406-0053-5507-8989-3c1cd6e8d4e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054436 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{ p,q \\}$ with $\\{ 4,9 \\},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/69/69ff8391a11b7bfa221b6b303f31551288bd969bdeed778fb05d3b7094e027ae.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{ (p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A \\times B| = |A| \\cdot |B| = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A \\times B) \\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f4a74a98f9df15273d59131eadc1155ff2d1105e1027e7d10b7e2366102e046.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A \\times B) \\times B| = |A \\times B| \\cdot |B| = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054437 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054437 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C = \\{ 3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D  =\\{ 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\},$ what is the cardinality of $C \\times D?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "677fc67f-94df-57a7-a804-22337acda702" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054437 #edb/ref 1074 "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D,$ the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n| C \\times D | = |C| \\cdot |D|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|C\\times D| & =|C|\\cdot |D| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054438 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054438 #edb/ref 1071 "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bc77b3d-fca6-5f66-ac12-e9fedcce15bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054438 #edb/ref 1074 "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A^2 = A \\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](images/46/4646235c2009dc45d7d09a52162ec0bdf929d122f450791ae9917b3ad8ef5a43.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c9647361885913d2534fefb49a878c2e365cf5be36d1bb4c059d95344dd1a60.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054633 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218605463